WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.421
A marketplace team had to moderate listings.

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:09.203
Check the text, check the image, check the price, and then decide.

00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:12.975
Someone suggested an agent with four tools.

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:14.497
The demo looked great.

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:20.411
In production, every now and then, the agent decided the image looked fine, and

00:00:20.411 --> 00:00:22.276
never called the image tool.

00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:24.621
Banned listings went live.

00:00:25.271 --> 00:00:27.877
The moderation path was always the same.

00:00:28.177 --> 00:00:30.917
Text, image, price, decision.

00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.784
The team gave the model a freedom the task never asked for.

00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:39.315
In this lesson, you'll learn the one question that would have prevented this, and

00:00:39.315 --> 00:00:42.083
how to pick the lowest rung that solves your problem.

00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:48.862
Every system built on a language model answers one design question, whether the team

00:00:48.862 --> 00:00:49.858
noticed or not.

00:00:50.158 --> 00:00:51.701
Who decides the next step?

00:00:52.001 --> 00:00:53.643
Your code, or the model?

00:00:54.843 --> 00:00:56.564
On one side, a chain.

00:00:56.864 --> 00:01:00.469
A fixed sequence of calls, with the path written in code.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:03.924
Step B always runs after step A.

00:01:04.224 --> 00:01:05.217
Always.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:07.793
On the other side, an agent.

00:01:08.093 --> 00:01:11.027
The model gets a goal and some tools.

00:01:11.327 --> 00:01:13.746
And on every turn, it decides what to do.

00:01:14.046 --> 00:01:17.570
Call a tool, which one, with which arguments, or stop.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.412
Between those two extremes sit workflows.

00:01:21.712 --> 00:01:26.343
Flows defined in code, that use the model at specific decision points.

00:01:26.643 --> 00:01:30.113
A router choosing among three fixed paths is a workflow.

00:01:30.413 --> 00:01:32.296
Code still owns the structure.

00:01:32.596 --> 00:01:35.314
The model only picks among options you designed.

00:01:35.964 --> 00:01:39.585
So the listing moderation was a four box chain.

00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:43.578
Turning it into an agent didn't add intelligence.

00:01:43.878 --> 00:01:46.397
It added permission to skip the image box.

00:01:47.597 --> 00:01:50.565
Think of it as a ladder, with four rungs.

00:01:51.215 --> 00:01:53.422
At the bottom, a single call.

00:01:53.722 --> 00:01:56.841
Nobody decides any path, it's one step.

00:01:57.141 --> 00:01:59.148
Classifying a ticket fits here.

00:01:59.798 --> 00:02:00.508
Then a chain.

00:02:00.808 --> 00:02:03.500
Code decides, and it's always the same.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.544
Extract, validate, summarize.

00:02:07.194 --> 00:02:08.212
Then a workflow.

00:02:08.512 --> 00:02:12.067
Code decides, with model choices at marked points.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:14.483
Routing is the classic example.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:17.049
And at the top, an agent.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:19.778
The model decides, every turn.

00:02:20.078 --> 00:02:25.109
Like fixing a bug in a repository, where the path depends on what you discover along

00:02:25.109 --> 00:02:25.588
the way.

00:02:26.238 --> 00:02:30.450
The rule: climb a rung only when the one below can't handle the job.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.070
Every rung up brings more flexibility.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:38.825
And also more cost, more latency, more variation, and more difficulty testing.

00:02:39.475 --> 00:02:44.494
Anthropic's guide on building effective agents, from December twenty twenty-four,

00:02:44.494 --> 00:02:45.733
draws the same line.

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:51.434
Start with workflows, where code orchestrates and the model fills the points of

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:52.254
uncertainty.

00:02:52.554 --> 00:02:55.644
Bring in an agent when you can't write the sequence in advance.

00:02:56.844 --> 00:02:58.495
So here's the test.

00:02:58.795 --> 00:02:59.673
One question.

00:02:59.973 --> 00:03:03.541
Can I draw the flowchart of this task before it runs?

00:03:04.191 --> 00:03:07.497
If yes, and it's always the same, it's a chain.

00:03:07.797 --> 00:03:10.001
Listing moderation passes easily.

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:12.546
Four boxes, no surprises.

00:03:13.196 --> 00:03:16.693
If yes, but with known branches, it's a workflow.

00:03:16.993 --> 00:03:19.271
An order bot is usually like this.

00:03:19.571 --> 00:03:22.890
If the message cites an order number, look up the order.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.814
If it's about a bill, go to finance.

00:03:26.114 --> 00:03:29.336
If it doesn't fit anything, hand off to a person.

00:03:29.636 --> 00:03:31.665
The branches exist before the run.

00:03:32.315 --> 00:03:38.190
And if no, because the next step depends on the previous result in ways you can't

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.003
predict, that's an agent.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:45.104
Investigating why a deploy broke on a Friday night rarely has a flowchart.

00:03:45.404 --> 00:03:48.454
The next command depends on the log that just appeared.

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:51.178
That's where an agent pays for itself.

00:03:51.828 --> 00:03:54.057
Notice it's not about importance.

00:03:54.357 --> 00:03:58.047
Banning a listing is important too, and it asks for a chain.

00:03:58.347 --> 00:04:01.070
Importance asks for control, not freedom.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:03.744
Here's one for you.

00:04:04.044 --> 00:04:09.405
You measured that the moderation agent calls the image tool on seventy percent of

00:04:09.405 --> 00:04:09.736
runs.

00:04:10.036 --> 00:04:12.946
The policy requires one hundred percent.

00:04:13.246 --> 00:04:15.193
What change closes the hole?

00:04:18.243 --> 00:04:23.760
Move the image call into code, before the decision, and let the model only judge the

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:24.220
result.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.650
That seventy percent is the model using a freedom the policy never granted.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.507
Raising the step limit wouldn't help.

00:04:32.807 --> 00:04:36.707
A limit prevents an endless loop, it doesn't require a step.

00:04:37.007 --> 00:04:42.395
And a prompt that says never skip the image lowers the skip rate, but never to zero.

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:46.762
The skip really disappears when it stops being the model's decision.

00:04:47.962 --> 00:04:49.467
Let's put a rough number on it.

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:55.084
Say a moderation flow has three checks, each around a thousand input tokens.

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:58.505
In a chain, that's always three calls.

00:04:59.155 --> 00:05:04.396
In an agent, the model may make two, or seven, if it gets unsure and re-reads.

00:05:05.046 --> 00:05:08.617
You trade a predictable cost for a cost with a tail.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:10.837
And testing changes too.

00:05:11.137 --> 00:05:16.340
A chain has three contracts, and its eval is simple: did all three steps pass?

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.367
An agent has a goal and a whole space of possible paths.

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:26.246
Its eval needs traces, a step limit, and test cases where skipping a step was the

00:05:26.246 --> 00:05:26.798
failure.

00:05:27.998 --> 00:05:32.960
The most common mistake in practice has a name: agent where a chain would do.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.023
Autonomy for a problem that never asked for it.

00:05:36.673 --> 00:05:37.893
Here's how it looks.

00:05:38.193 --> 00:05:42.258
A team has an agent with twelve tools to onboard new merchants.

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:47.579
And in practice, it always asks for the same four documents, in the same order.

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:49.387
That's not a coincidence.

00:05:49.687 --> 00:05:51.011
It's evidence.

00:05:51.311 --> 00:05:54.814
The flowchart already exists, written by the traces.

00:05:55.114 --> 00:05:56.154
Put it in code.

00:05:56.804 --> 00:05:58.789
And the opposite mistake exists too.

00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:04.615
A chain with thirty if statements, trying to predict every question a fraud analyst

00:06:04.615 --> 00:06:05.281
might ask.

00:06:05.581 --> 00:06:08.829
Each new if is a branch you invented without data.

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:12.713
When the branches explode, the workflow got too small.

00:06:13.013 --> 00:06:14.169
That's when you climb.

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:19.396
But you climb with an eval, built from the cases the chain no longer covers, and

00:06:19.396 --> 00:06:21.366
with a cost limit for the agent.

00:06:22.566 --> 00:06:25.016
The same cut shows up in every stack.

00:06:25.316 --> 00:06:30.366
In LangGraph, a chain or a workflow is a graph whose edges you write.

00:06:30.666 --> 00:06:31.939
An agent is a tool loop.

00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:38.311
The agent helpers in LangChain, the OpenAI Agents SDK and the Claude Agent SDK all

00:06:38.311 --> 00:06:39.866
start from that loop.

00:06:40.516 --> 00:06:42.675
But the library doesn't pick the rung.

00:06:42.975 --> 00:06:43.718
You do.

00:06:44.018 --> 00:06:49.210
If the flowchart fits on paper, your code draws the edges, whatever library you use.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:54.801
Now take three tasks from your own product and classify each one.

00:06:55.101 --> 00:06:55.884
Fixed flowchart?

00:06:56.184 --> 00:06:56.929
Branches?

00:06:57.229 --> 00:06:57.994
Which rung?

00:06:58.644 --> 00:07:04.078
Summarizing a meeting has a fixed flowchart, no branches.

00:07:04.378 --> 00:07:05.656
That makes it a chain.

00:07:06.306 --> 00:07:10.944
Answering a customer about an order has a fixed flowchart, with branches.

00:07:11.244 --> 00:07:11.903
Workflow.

00:07:12.553 --> 00:07:16.499
Investigating why a deploy failed has no fixed flowchart.

00:07:16.799 --> 00:07:17.470
Agent.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.856
If a task you already solve with an agent landed on chain or workflow, you just

00:07:22.856 --> 00:07:26.933
found a candidate for a refactoring called replace agent with chain.

00:07:28.133 --> 00:07:29.931
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:30.231 --> 00:07:32.062
Autonomy isn't a feature.

00:07:32.362 --> 00:07:36.255
It's a cost you pay when you can't write the path in advance.

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:39.646
Start at the lowest rung that solves the problem.

00:07:39.946 --> 00:07:43.828
You can always climb later, with an eval showing it was worth it.

00:07:44.478 --> 00:07:49.352
In the next lesson, we'll build the first rung properly: prompt chaining, a few

00:07:49.352 --> 00:07:51.512
short steps, each one easy to test.

00:07:51.812 --> 00:07:52.641
See you there.
